-      "id": "fix-error-cycles",
-      "sha": "b48a9c63064b782fe5e9bb86329603da6d0835cc",
-      "parentSha": "909d452917cdd370b63a970aa6e596285cca71b3",
-      "spec": "Implement memory-leak prevention in GraphQL error handling by clearing reference cycles and ensuring internal GraphQL objects don’t retain error references.\n\nRequired changes:\n\n1) Create a new module to clear exception reference cycles\n- File: saleor/graphql/error.py\n- Add functions that clear locals on traceback frames and recursively clear nested exceptions:\n  - clear_traceback_locals(tb): iterate across connected frames in tb.tb_frame via f_back, and call frame.clear(), ignoring RuntimeError for executing frames.\n  - clear_exception_locals(exception): if exception.__traceback__ exists, clear it via clear_traceback_locals; recursively handle exception.__cause__; if the exception is a GraphQLError and has original_error, clear that original error as well.\n  - clear_errors(errors): iterate over each error object in the provided list and apply clear_exception_locals.\n\n2) Move and extend GraphQL-Core patching\n- Remove the old executor patch module:\n  - Delete file: saleor/graphql/executor.py\n- Create a new module for GraphQL-Core patches:\n  - File: saleor/graphql/graphql_core.py\n  - Recreate patch_executor() to wrap graphql.execution.executor.complete_value_catching_error so that each resolution occurs inside saleor.core.db.connection.allow_writer_in_context(info.context), preserving existing behavior for mutations vs queries.\n  - Add patch_execution_context(): set executor.ExecutionContext.__del__ to a function that deletes the errors attribute (if present) upon object deletion to avoid reference cycles.\n  - Add patch_execution_result(): set graphql.execution.base.ExecutionResult.__del__ to a function that deletes the errors attribute to break reference cycles.\n\n3) Integrate error clearing in GraphQL view\n- File: saleor/graphql/views.py\n- Import clear_errors from .error.\n- After populating response[\"errors\"] (i.e., after formatting ExecutionResult.errors), call clear_errors(execution_result.errors) to break reference cycles in error objects coming from GraphQL-Core-Legacy.\n\n4) Initialize GraphQL-Core patches in settings\n- File: saleor/settings.py\n- Replace import of patch_executor from .graphql.executor with import from .graphql.graphql_core: patch_executor, patch_execution_context, patch_execution_result.\n- After existing patch calls (e.g., patch_local), call patch_execution_context() and patch_execution_result() to ensure these patches are applied at startup.\n\n5) Add garbage-collection tests for error scenarios\n- File: saleor/graphql/core/tests/garbage_collection/test_errors.py\n- Add tests grouped with @pytest.mark.xdist_group(name=\"garbage_collection\") to serialize them:\n  - test_permission_error: run a products query requiring permissions; assert gc.garbage == [] after gc.collect(); assert returned error code is PermissionDenied and data contains expected product name.\n  - test_query_cost_error: with @override_settings(GRAPHQL_QUERY_MAX_COMPLEXITY=5), run a products query exceeding cost; assert gc.garbage == [] and error code is QueryCostError.\n  - test_exception_in_resolver: mock saleor.graphql.product.schema.resolve_products to raise GraphQLError; assert gc.garbage == [] and error code is GraphQLError.\n  - test_exception_in_dataloader: mock saleor.graphql.product.schema.ChannelBySlugLoader.batch_load to raise GraphQLError; assert gc.garbage == [] and error code is GraphQLError.\n  - test_input_validation_error: send invalid variables to ProductVariantStocksUpdate; assert gc.garbage == [] and error code is GraphQLError.\n- In each test, disable automatic GC and set debugging via disable_gc_for_garbage_collection_test() before request, enforce gc.collect() after request, and restore GC with clean_up_after_garbage_collection_test() in finally.\n- Use rf, GraphQLView(backend=backend, schema=schema), JSON encoding with DjangoJSONEncoder, and get_graphql_content(..., ignore_errors=True).\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- Execution of GraphQL queries/mutations that produce errors should not leave any objects in gc.garbage after a forced collection within the tests.\n- ExecutionResult and ExecutionContext no longer retain lingering references to error objects post-response.\n- The GraphQLView integrates error clearing without changing the outward API besides memory behavior.\n- Settings successfully apply all patches at startup.\n- All new tests pass and existing behavior for mutation DB writer access remains unchanged.",
-      "prompt": "Improve GraphQL error handling to prevent memory leaks caused by reference cycles. Add a mechanism that clears locals from exception tracebacks and recursively clears nested exceptions. Patch GraphQL-Core’s execution context and results so that error references are dropped on deletion. Ensure the GraphQL view clears errors after formatting them. Initialize these patches during application startup. Add tests covering several error scenarios (permission, query cost, resolver and dataloader exceptions, input validation) and assert that forced garbage collection leaves no garbage. Keep existing behavior for DB writer access in mutations intact.",
