-      "spec": "Add cache invalidation for stored payment methods in transaction flows.\n\nWhat to implement:\n1) Utility helper in saleor/payment/utils.py\n- Implement invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods_if_needed(event, source_object, app_identifier):\n  - If event.type is one of AUTHORIZATION_REQUEST, AUTHORIZATION_SUCCESS, CHARGE_REQUEST, CHARGE_SUCCESS AND source_object.user_id is set, then invalidate the LIST_STORED_PAYMENT_METHODS cache for the specific user/channel/app by calling invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods(user_id=source_object.user_id, channel_slug=source_object.channel.slug, app_identifier=app_identifier) from saleor/webhook/transport/list_stored_payment_methods.\n  - Otherwise do nothing.\n\n2) Integrate helper into payment utilities in saleor/payment/utils.py\n- In create_transaction_event_from_request_and_webhook_response(...): after the event has been created and amounts updated, compute source_object = transaction_item.checkout or transaction_item.order; if both event and source_object exist, call invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods_if_needed(event, source_object, app.identifier).\n- In handle_transaction_initialize_session(...): after obtaining created_event from create_transaction_event_from_request_and_webhook_response, call invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods_if_needed(created_event, source_object, app.identifier) before returning.\n- In handle_transaction_process_session(...): likewise, call invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods_if_needed(created_event, source_object, app.identifier) before returning.\n\n3) Update GraphQL mutation for event reporting\n- In saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/transaction/transaction_event_report.py:\n  - Import invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods_if_needed from saleor/payment/utils.py.\n  - Early in perform_mutation, compute source_object = transaction.order or transaction.checkout.\n  - After transaction save/update logic, set app_identifier = provided app_identifier or transaction.app_identifier. If both app_identifier and source_object exist, call invalidate_cache_for_stored_payment_methods_if_needed(transaction_event, source_object, app_identifier) before returning the mutation payload.\n\n4) Tests for correct invalidation behavior\n- GraphQL transaction_event_report, transaction_initialize, and transaction_process tests:\n  - For both order and checkout contexts, parametrize over event results AUTHORIZATION_REQUEST, AUTHORIZATION_SUCCESS, CHARGE_REQUEST, CHARGE_SUCCESS and assert cache invalidation is called exactly once.\n  - For AUTHORIZATION_FAILURE, CHARGE_ACTION_REQUIRED, CHARGE_FAILURE, assert the cache is not invalidated.\n  - Ensure the app has LIST_STORED_PAYMENT_METHODS webhook configured, with its identifier set, and associate a user with the order/checkout. Use generate_cache_key_for_webhook and WebhookEventSyncType.LIST_STORED_PAYMENT_METHODS to build the expected key for assertions.\n  - Mock saleor.webhook.transport.list_stored_payment_methods.cache.delete and assert it's called with the generated key (or not called for negative cases).\n- Payment utils tests:\n  - In create_transaction_event_from_request_and_webhook_response flows for order and checkout, verify invalidation on CHARGE_SUCCESS and no invalidation on CHARGE_FAILURE with analogous setup and assertions.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- Cache invalidation only occurs for the specified event types and when source object has a user.\n- Invalidation targets the specific user, channel, and app that handled the transaction.\n- Tests cover both positive and negative paths for order and checkout, constructing and asserting the correct cache key.",
-      "prompt": "Implement cache invalidation for stored payment methods across the transactions API. When a payment app initializes, processes, or reports a transaction event that could store or change a payment method, invalidate the cached stored payment methods for the specific user, channel, and app. Only invalidate for authorization/charge request or success results, not for failures or action-required outcomes, and only when the order/checkout has a user. Integrate this into the transaction initialize/process flows, the event reporting mutation, and the utility that creates transaction events from webhook responses. Provide tests that mock cache deletion, set up a webhook for listing stored payment methods, and verify that the expected cache key is deleted for both order and checkout paths, and that no deletion occurs for non-applicable results.",
