-      "spec": "Implement a refactor that extracts shared batching and attribute search vector logic into reusable utilities and updates all call sites accordingly.\n\nScope and required changes:\n\n1) Add a core batching utility\n- File to add: saleor/core/utils/batches.py\n- Add function queryset_in_batches(queryset: QuerySet, batch_size: int) that:\n  - Iterates a queryset in ascending pk order in batches of size batch_size.\n  - Uses pk__gt start_pk to window slices; for each loop, fetch values_list(\"pk\", flat=True) from queryset.filter(pk__gt=start_pk).order_by(\"pk\")[:batch_size].\n  - Yields lists of primary keys for each batch until no more results, updating start_pk to the last pk from the current batch.\n\n2) Add attribute search utilities\n- File to add: saleor/attribute/search.py\n- Add function get_search_vectors_for_attribute_values(attribute, values, page_id_to_title_map=None, weight=\"B\") -> list[NoValidationSearchVector]:\n  - Build search vectors for assigned attribute values according to AttributeInputType with config=\"simple\" and the given weight.\n  - DROPDOWN and MULTISELECT: use value.name.\n  - RICH_TEXT: use clean_editor_js(value.rich_text, to_string=True).\n  - PLAIN_TEXT: use value.plain_text.\n  - NUMERIC: concatenate value.name and attribute.unit separated by a single space if a unit exists; else just value.name.\n  - DATE and DATE_TIME: use value.date_time formatted as \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\".\n  - REFERENCE and SINGLE_REFERENCE: only PAGE references are supported; include items where reference_page_id is not null and map to a search string using get_reference_attribute_search_value().\n- Add helper get_reference_attribute_search_value(attribute_value, page_id_to_title_map=None) -> str that returns:\n  - If attribute_value.reference_page_id is set: page title from page_id_to_title_map when provided; otherwise, attribute_value.reference_page.title when available; else empty string.\n  - Empty string when no reference_page_id.\n\n3) Update product search to use the new utilities\n- File to modify: saleor/product/search.py\n- Replace the local batching function with an import: from ..core.utils.batches import queryset_in_batches, and update update_products_search_vector to call queryset_in_batches(products, PRODUCTS_BATCH_SIZE).\n- Replace local attribute vector builder and helper:\n  - Import get_search_vectors_for_attribute_values from ..attribute.search.\n  - In generate_attributes_search_vector_value, replace calls to the removed get_search_vectors_for_values with get_search_vectors_for_attribute_values(attribute, values, page_id_to_title_map=page_id_to_title_map, weight=\"B\").\n  - In generate_attributes_search_vector_value_with_assignment, replace calls to the removed get_search_vectors_for_values with get_search_vectors_for_attribute_values(attribute, values, weight=\"B\").\n- Remove now-redundant local functions: queryset_in_batches, get_search_vectors_for_values, and get_reference_attribute_search_value, along with any imports that are no longer needed (e.g., Attribute, AttributeInputType, AttributeValue, clean_editor_js, Value) if solely used by the removed functions.\n- Ensure PRODUCT_SEARCH_FIELDS logic and other behavior remain unchanged.\n\n4) Update CSV export batching to use the new utility\n- File to modify: saleor/csv/utils/export.py\n- Import queryset_in_batches from ...core.utils.batches.\n- Update loops to call queryset_in_batches(queryset, BATCH_SIZE) in:\n  - export_products_in_batches\n  - export_gift_cards_in_batches\n  - export_voucher_codes_in_batches\n- Remove the local queryset_in_batches function from this module.\n- Preserve existing BATCH_SIZE in this module.\n\n5) Update GraphQL attribute value update mutation to batch and lock correctly\n- File to modify: saleor/graphql/attribute/mutations/attribute_value_update.py\n- Import queryset_in_batches from ....core.utils.batches and product_qs_select_for_update from ....product.lock_objects.\n- Define BATCH_SIZE = 10000 (module-level) to control batch size for updates.\n- Remove the local PRODUCTS_BATCH_SIZE constant and the local queryset_in_batches function.\n- Refactor post_save_action to:\n  - Call a new classmethod mark_search_index_dirty(instance) to mark affected products instead of performing the update inline.\n  - Then call the existing plugin manager events for attribute value and attribute updates.\n- Implement mark_search_index_dirty(instance) to:\n  - Build a queryset of product_models.Product with search_index_dirty=False and either a direct value assignment (Exists over instance.productvalueassignment) or an indirect assignment via variants (Exists over a variants queryset filtered by instance.variantassignments), ordered by pk.\n  - Iterate over queryset_in_batches(products, BATCH_SIZE); for each batch:\n    - Open a new transaction.atomic() block.\n    - Lock rows in a consistent order using product_qs_select_for_update().filter(pk__in=batch_pks) to avoid deadlocks.\n    - Update product_models.Product.objects.filter(pk__in=batch_pks).update(search_index_dirty=True).\n\nExpected behavior after refactor:\n- Product search vectors continue to be generated as before, but attribute-value-derived vectors and reference resolution come from saleor/attribute/search.py with explicit weight control.\n- All code paths use the centralized queryset_in_batches utility with per-call-site batch sizes, avoiding duplicate implementations.\n- Marking products for reindex after attribute value updates proceeds in batches with explicit row locking, reducing deadlock risk and maintaining consistent lock ordering.\n- No changes to public APIs or database schemas; refactor is internal and preserves functional parity.",
