-      "spec": "Implement warehouse-based filtering for orders via fulfillment data.\n\nScope of changes:\n1) GraphQL order filtering (Python):\n- In saleor/graphql/order/filters.py:\n  - Add necessary imports to support typing and model traversal for warehouses used in fulfillments (Mapping, QuerySet; FulfillmentLine, Stock, Warehouse).\n  - Implement a helper that, given a fulfillment queryset and warehouse filter input, restricts fulfillments to those that have at least one line whose stock belongs to a warehouse matching the input. The input supports filtering by:\n    - id (GlobalIDFilterInput)\n    - slug (StringFilterInput)\n    - external_reference (StringFilterInput)\n    The helper must:\n    - Return an empty queryset when no warehouse filter values are provided.\n    - Combine id/slug/external_reference constraints on Warehouse appropriately.\n    - Narrow Stocks by matching the Warehouse(s).\n    - Narrow FulfillmentLines by matching the Stock(s).\n    - Narrow fulfillments to those referenced by those FulfillmentLines.\n    - Use the existing queryset (db alias, filters) when further constraining the fulfillment queryset rather than constructing a fresh queryset unless needed.\n  - Extend the existing filter_fulfillments method to recognize a new warehouse field inside each fulfillment filter object. Within one fulfillment filter object, status/metadata/warehouse conditions must combine (logical AND). Across multiple fulfillment objects in the list, combine with OR semantics, then apply as an Exists subquery against orders (so that orders are included if any of their fulfillments match the composite conditions).\n\n2) GraphQL schema:\n- In saleor/graphql/schema.graphql:\n  - Add a new input type FulfillmentWarehouseFilterInput with fields:\n    - id: GlobalIDFilterInput\n    - slug: StringFilterInput\n    - externalReference: StringFilterInput\n  - Extend FulfillmentFilterInput to include a new field:\n    - warehouse: FulfillmentWarehouseFilterInput\n  - Ensure descriptions categorize under Orders and match the style of existing schema descriptions.\n\n3) Filter utilities typing:\n- In saleor/graphql/utils/filters.py:\n  - Broaden type hints for helper filter functions so they accept generic Mapping/Sequence rather than concrete dict/list, for:\n    - filter_where_by_value_field: value parameter should support Mapping[str, scalar or Sequence of scalar]\n    - filter_where_by_id_field: value parameter should support Mapping[str, str or list[str]]\n  - No change in runtime behavior is required; only broadened typing/acceptance semantics.\n\n4) Tests:\n- In saleor/graphql/order/tests/queries/test_order_with_where.py:\n  - Update the orders_with_fulfillments fixture to create:\n    - Two Stocks for two different product variants, each in a distinct Warehouse.\n    - One Fulfillment per order with FulfillmentLines pointing to those Stocks (no allocations needed for this test).\n  - Add test cases that validate warehouse filtering inside where.fulfillments:\n    - Filter by warehouse.id using eq and oneOf.\n    - Filter by warehouse.slug using eq and oneOf.\n    - Filter by warehouse.externalReference using eq and oneOf.\n    - Verify that non-existing id/slug/externalReference inputs yield zero results.\n    - Verify combining warehouse with additional fulfillment filters (status and metadata) both in matching and non-matching scenarios.\n  - Use to_global_id_or_none when passing warehouse ids in variables.\n\n5) Documentation note:\n- In CHANGELOG.md under the section describing new order filters, add a bullet noting: \"Filter by warehouse used to fulfill the order.\"\n\nBehavioral expectations:\n- The orders query, when provided with where.fulfillments containing a warehouse filter, returns only those orders that have at least one fulfillment line whose stock belongs to a warehouse matching the provided filters.\n- Within a single where.fulfillments entry, the status, metadata, and warehouse constraints must all match (AND). When multiple entries are provided in the array, they are combined as OR.\n- The new warehouse-based filter composes correctly with existing where-based filters for fulfillments and other order attributes.\n- Empty or invalid warehouse filters result in no orders being returned for that particular fulfillment filter object.\n- The schema changes expose the new input fields in a manner consistent with existing naming conventions (externalReference camelCase in schema; mapped to external_reference in model-level filters).",
-      "prompt": "Add support for filtering orders by the warehouses used to fulfill them. Extend the existing fulfillment filters in the orders where-based filtering so that a warehouse filter can be supplied with id, slug, or externalReference. Make sure this composes correctly with the existing fulfillment status and metadata filters and that it affects the orders query by including orders that have any fulfillment whose lines reference stock in warehouses matching the filter. Update the GraphQL schema to include a warehouse filter input for fulfillments, broaden the filter utility typings to accept mappings/sequences, and add tests that cover id, slug, externalReference, non-matching cases, and combinations with other fulfillment filters. Also update the changelog to mention this new filtering capability.",
